# SRG-APP-000016-WSR-000005  -  CAT II
# The web server must generate information to be used by external
# applications or entities to monitor and control remote access.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000016-WSR-000005
group:           V-206354
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-000067
control_rev5:    AC-17

contribution:    Splunk produces the remote-access records an external monitor consumes:
                 Splunk Web writes an HTTP access log naming the client and request,
                 splunkd writes authentication and session events to the audit trail, and
                 audit.conf controls whether those audit events reach an index where an
                 external monitoring application can retrieve them; outputs.conf [tcpout]
                 is the application's means of pushing copies to a destination the
                 organization names.

governs:         audit.conf - The audit trail Splunk keeps of its own activity, and how those records
                 are signed.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           audit.conf [auditTrail] queueing not_equals false
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] log.access_file https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] log.access_file non_empty
                 reason: "log.access_file = <filename>. The HTTP access log filename. This
                         file is written in the default $SPLUNK_HOME/var/log directory.
                         Default: web_access.log" Related shipped settings:
                         log.access_maxsize = 25000000 and log.access_maxfiles = 5.
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The external application or entity that monitors and controls remote
                 access is a separate component; Splunk can generate and forward the
                 records but cannot act on them, and the check text's "mechanism ... to
                 monitor and control access" is completed by that consumer.
external:        Where the organization directs copies to that consumer over the network,
                 the destination addresses configured in outputs.conf [tcpout] and the
                 reachability of the receiver are deployment facts the application cannot
                 verify.
external:        If audit queueing is deliberately set to false, an inputs.conf monitor
                 stanza tailing the audit log carries the delivery instead, as the spec
                 states.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/web.conf.spec:871-874 (log.access_file, default
                 web_access.log); splunk-spec-files/audit.conf.spec:23-29 ([auditTrail]
                 queueing, default true; false requires an inputs.conf tail);
                 splunk-spec-files/outputs.conf.spec:61-71 ([tcpout], defaultGroup);
                 SRG-APP-000016-WSR-000005 check and fix text.
finding:         Splunk does not generate the remote-access information an external
                 monitoring application would consume, because web.conf [settings]
                 log.access_file names no HTTP access log or audit.conf [auditTrail]
                 queueing is set to false with no compensating monitor input, leaving audit
                 events unindexed; this is assessed at the application layer alone, and the
                 external monitoring and access-control application itself, together with
                 its network delivery, is recorded as an external dependency rather than
                 treated as a Splunk setting.
